ll-liquid surface (not any crust that's formed over it — crust reads as safe, walkable ground regardless of which liquid reacted to form it) call `take_hit()` on the player each frame of contact, and the player swims in a hazardous basin's liquid with buoyancy and takes damage each frame. The settled body draws as a real per-column staircase polygon (crust between its two stored faces, liquid from its own floor-anchored surface down to the seabed, distinct colors for each; any gap between them after a drain is a void and is simply not drawn; a skinned liquid's body is drawn as one filled rectangle per ripple strip, not a polygon, because `draw_colored_polygon()` triangulates all-or-nothing and a ripple's whole-pixel staircase could occasionally be rejected, blanking the whole body for a frame — unskinned liquid and crust, whose tops are flat, still draw polygons, and `debug_check_body` on a basin warns whenever the polygon form of the body would fail to triangulate); slag, water and every crust are skinned with art from `sprites/liquids/` (per liquid type: `SKIN_BY_TYPE` for the surface, with water looping faster than slag, and `CRUST_SKIN_PATH_BY_TYPE` for the crust, so water can cool from water colour; a missing file falls back to flat colour / the slag crust art) — the art is the top layer; a liquid's body below it is the art's bottom-row colour, while crust art has its non-neutral fringe cut transparent so the liquid shows through it, or over a drained void only the fringe inside the crust is filled neutral and the underside keeps the art's own jagged edge (see `LiquidSkin` and the Skins note and Crust rendering in `water-mechanic.md`); the body, surface art and crust also extend `wall_overlap_px` behind each wall column so a transparent strip on a wall sprite shows no gap (see Wall overlap in `water-mechanic.md`); everything is drawn behind the tilemap so any approximation at a sloped tile's edge stays hidden. Moving particles push the player horizontally within a small radius of them (`PLAYER_PUSH_RADIUS` — currently reads too large, flagged for retuning), summed by direction and capped both by a flat ceiling and by whichever contributing particle is currently fastest, so a crowd of slow particles can't shove the player faster than the liquid is actually moving. `WaterFaucet` is tile-anchored (node origin at its tile's top-left, `spawn_offset` picks the spawn point inside the tile) and emits particles at a per-instance `flow_rate` (volume per second, independent of the particles-per-tile preset) while `is_open`. Sitting on the floor tile of a basin's interior column, it is a **drain**: each drip debits `PARTICLE_VOLUME` from that basin's `fill_volume` and spawns a particle of the basin's liquid type, leaving any crust hanging over the void it opens; nothing comes out once no liquid lies over the tile. Anywhere else it is a plain **source** dripping its own `liquid_type` (water or slag); the melt burst API (`spawn_melt_particles(tile_pos, liquid_type, world_center = INF, cell_rotation = 0)`, covering both ice→water and metal→slag) is called by `MeltableBlock._melt()` once per 16px cell it covers; with a finite `world_center` the burst is spread over the cell in the block's rotated frame, inset by `SPAWN_INSET` (0.85) so every particle starts inside the block's volume, otherwise over the snapped, axis-aligned tile. **Carry and load**: a particle resting on a registered body is carried by that body's whole transform change: each frame `_solid_motion` maps every body whose `global_transform` changed (`_registered_solid_prev_xform` holds last frame's) to `new × old.affine_inverse()`, and a resting particle's position is mapped through it, so a platform's translation carries it and the see-saw's rotation swings it round the pivot; particle velocity is untouched. `forget_motion(body)` resets a body's stored transform so a teleport (a checkpoint restore) isn't carried. A resting particle on a registered body that has `get_pivot_global()` (a `RotatingBody`) also loads it: `LiquidProfile.weight_per_tile_volume` (block-mass units per tile of volume: water 1.0, the same as ice; slag 3.0, under metal's 4; steam 0) × `PARTICLE_VOLUME` × the particle's horizontal offset from the pivot, summed per body each frame and read through `get_resting_torque(body)`; particles on a basin's synthesized surface add nothing. **Surface velocity**: `WaterBasin.get_liquid_surface_velocity_y()` (px/s, negative = rising) is what a floating block rides. It is estimated from the change of `get_liquid_surface_world_y()` between physics frames, smoothed over `SURFACE_VELOCITY_SMOOTH_TIME` (0.25 s, since fill arrives in particle-sized steps) and clamped to ±`SURFACE_VELOCITY_MAX` (240), lazily and once per physics frame however many bodies ask; the first query, or one after more than `SURFACE_VELOCITY_GAP_FRAMES` (3) frames without a query, reads 0 and restarts the estimate, so a restore or a teleported fill can't read as a flood. Runtime scratch only (`_surface_vy`, `_surface_prev_y`, `_surface_vy_frame`), never saved; ripple isn't part of it. **Surface ripple** (cosmetic, in `basin.gd`): a per-basin wave simulation over 4px samples bends only the drawn top edge of the settled liquid, driven by `WaterBasin.disturb(world_x, velocity, radius)` from landing particles, drain drips, player swim entry/wake and ice/metal block splashes. Viscosity is per-liquid data in `LiquidProfile` (`ripple_speed`, `ripple_damping`, `ripple_strength`): slag's waves are slow, heavily damped and weakly stirred. It costs nothing while calm and never affects `fill_volume`, queries, collision or crust. See `water-mechanic.md` for the full data-flow/API/tuning-constant reference and what's still open. A surfing player reads the liquid through two pull queries, `get_floor_wetness()` and `find_skim_basin()`, and a basin raises a runtime one-way skim floor for it on layer 3 (see the Water bullet under Surf and `water-mechanic.md`). **Laser boiling** (roadmap 5.4): `WaterManager.boil_along_beam(points, delta)` boils the WATER of every `boilable` basin a beam polyline crosses, emitting steam at the crossing and draining the basin at its `boil_volume_scale` (see Laser turret); `BOIL_STEAM_VOLUME_PER_SEC`, `BOIL_DRAIN_VOLUME_PER_SEC` and `BOIL_ENTRY_NUDGE` set its rates. `WaterBasin` gains the authored exports `boilable` and `boil_volume_scale`. **Electrified water**: a Spark (see Rail bot) or an Arc (see Arc turret) reaching the settled surface of a crust-free WATER basin electrifies the whole pool for `electrified_time` (default 1 s, exported; a later strike restarts the countdown, never adds to it). `WaterBasin.try_electrify_at(world_point)` is the only entry (it applies the refusals listed under Rail bot, then sets the runtime `_electrified_left`, kicks a cosmetic ripple through `disturb()`, plays `interactive/basin_zap` at the surface and returns true); `is_electrified()` reads it and `is_hazardous_now()` (a hazardous liquid, or electrified) is the one question the player's swim check and `WaterManager.find_skim_basin()` ask, so a future transient hazard goes there and nowhere else. `conducts_sparks` (default true) and `electrified_time` are authored exports; the countdown is runtime-only and **not saved**, so a restore comes back calm. While electrified the pool is hazardous like slag but only to a submerged player (`ELECTRIC_CONTACT_DAMAGE`, 1 per `take_hit()`, paced by its i-frames, in every held state), and a surfer cannot skim it (no skim floor is raised, so a board over it falls in, ends the surf and is hurt). It affects nothing else: floating blocks, flowing particles, the basin sensor and power circuits do not read it. The look is placeholder code in `_draw_electric()` (flickering tint, bright surface line, zigzag arcs, a bolt at the strike point, tapering over the last `ELECTRIC_FADE_TIME`); `_process` stays on while it runs.
 
 Reads with: `water-mechanic.md` (the full reference: data model, API, every constant), `player.md` (swim), `player-surf.md` (the skim floor, wetness, steam lift), `meltable-blocks.md` (melting, buoyancy), `rotating-bodies.md` (resting torque), `lasers-arcs-mirrors.md` (electrified water), `power.md` (the faucet, the basin sensor), `enemies.md` (the rail bot spark).
